<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meiningen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meiningen is a town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werra">Werra</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhon">Rhön</a>. Held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> and then the counts of Henneberg, it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> Wettins and from 1680 was the capital of the Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen. Its court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> under Duke George II became famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the late 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meiningen.jpg" alt="Meiningen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kramer96 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stadtkirche_St._Marien_in_Meiningen,_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
